[[File:Saito Sanemori Statue Menuma 01.jpg|thumb|240px|Saito Sanemori Statue]]
[[File:Kisoumon.jpg|thumb|240px|Kisoumon]]'''Kangiin'' is {{ill|lt=Saitama Prefecture|Q128186|qid=Q128186|1=Saitama Prefecture}}{{ill|lt=Kumagaya City|Q41106|qid=Q41106|1=Kumagaya}}{{jalink|Menuma Town|Q6817684| It is {{ill|lt=Buddhism|Q748|qid=Q748|1=Buddhism}}{{ill|lt=temple|Q5393308|qid=Q5393308|1=Buddhist temple}} of {{ill|lt=Koyasan Shingon sect|Q3077357|qid=Q3077357|1=Kōyasan Shingon-shū}} located in Menuma}}. {{ill|lt=Kankiten#Japan's three great holy deities|Q11545315|qid=Q11545315|lt=Japan's three great holy deities|1=Kangiten}} It is generally referred to as ``Menuma Shodenzan,'' which is a place name attached to the mountain name, and is mainly used in official guides.
Also, "Saitama {{ill|lt=Nikko Toshogu|Q696641|qid=Q696641|lt=Nikko|1=Nikkō Tōshō-gū}}" (before it was designated as a national treasure, it was "Saitama's Konikko") |publisher= local publisher |isbn= 4-87663-703-2 |date= 2004-09}}</ref> ). It is called ``Shoten-sama (of Menuma)'' by worshipers and local residents.

=== History ===
According to temple legend, in the 3rd year of Jisho ({{ill|lt=1179|Q19698|qid=Q19698|1=1179}}), the military commander {{ill|lt=Sanemori Saito|Q3472140|qid=Q3472140|lt=It is said that Fujibetsuto Sanemori|1=Sanemori Saitō}} built Shotengu Shrine to enshrine the guardian deity Daisei {{ill|lt=Kankiten|Q11545315|qid=Q11545315|1=Kangiten}} (Shoten) and made it the general guardian of Nagaisho. Later, in the 8th year of Kenkyu (1197), Ryo'o Sozu (Sanenaga, the second son of Saito Betto Sanemori) built Kangi-in Choraku-ji Temple as the Betto temple (honbo) of Shotengu Shrine, and it is said that {{ill|lt=Eleven-faced Kannon|Q11404731|qid=Q11404731|1=Ekādaśamukha}} was the principal image.

{{ill|lt=Kamakura Shogunate|Q736839|qid=Q736839|1=Kamakura shogunate}}'s first shogun, {{ill|lt=Minamoto no Yoritomo|Q242800|qid=Q242800|1=Minamoto no Yoritomo}}, visited the shrine, and in the medieval period it was protected by the lord of Oshi Castle, and in the early modern period it was rebuilt by {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Ieyasu|Q171977|qid=Q171977|1=Tokugawa Ieyasu}}, but it was destroyed by the Great Menuma Fire in 1670. The current Shotendo (main shrine) was rebuilt between the Kyoho and Horeki eras (mid-18th century).

Restoration work on the main shrine was carried out from 2003 to 2011, and the completion ceremony for the main building was held on January 18, 2010, and the completion ceremony was held on June 1, 2011, and it opened to the public on the same day. On July 9, 2012, Seitendo (main shrine) was designated as a {{ill|lt=National Treasure|Q1139795|qid=Q1139795|1=National Treasure (Japan)}} (the first designation as a National Treasure building in Saitama Prefecture).

=== Precincts ===

*Main shrine (Shotendo)
*: Restoration work from 2003 to 2011 brought back the brightly colored carvings from when the building was first built. The total construction cost was 1.35 billion yen. 960 million yen came from national, prefectural and city subsidies, and 350 million yen came from donations from believers.
*: An admission fee of 700 yen is required to visit the inner shrine (no admission fee is required for regular visits to the shrine).
