=== Cultural assets ===

=== National Treasure ===
; Seitendo (main hall)
: It is a mausoleum-style {{ill|lt=Gongen-zukuri|Q3155268|qid=Q3155268|1=Ishi-no-ma-zukuri}} building (an architectural style that combines multiple buildings, as seen in Nikko Toshogu Shrine, etc.), which consists of a worship hall, a middle hall (so-ma), and a back hall. The master carpenter was the master carpenter Hayashi Hyogo Masakiyo of Menuma, a descendant of {{ill|lt=Masanobu Hiranai|Q11482097|qid=Q11482097|1=Heinouchi Masanobu}}, the master carpenter of the shogunate.The building was rebuilt over a period of more than 20 years, from 1735 to 1760, by his son Masanobu. The inner hall is a gambrel-style structure with 3 girders and 3 girders, with a front facing prayer hall.The middle hall is Ryosage-zukuri, with 3 girder rows and 1 girder.The worship hall is gambrel-style, with 5 girder rows and 3 beams.These are connected to form a single building, and the roof is all roofed with tile-rod copper plates.: The inner and outer halls are highly decorative with splendid carvings, lacquering, coloring, metal fittings, etc. both inside and outside. The ``eagle and monkey'' carvings on the paneling on the south side of the inner hall are said to have been created by the legendary carving craftsman {{ill|lt=Hidashi Jingoro|Q2668220|qid=Q2668220|1=Hidari Jingorō}}, but the actual master carvers were Ginpachiro Ishihara (Ginpachi) and Bunjiro Sekiguchi. In the inner hall, the pillars, pushpieces, and other members are carved with ground patterns, and the large paneling under the inner law is decorated with the Seven Lucky Gods, and the area under the rim is decorated with colored carvings depicting the theme of playing with karako. Each of the carvings has a theme related to a Chinese story, such as ``Three Saints' Susan'' and ``{{ill|lt=Sima Guang|Q33566|qid=Q33566|lt=Sima Wengong|1=Sima Guang}}'s Bottle Breaker'' under the kara gable, and ``Kotogi Calligraphy'' under the kara gable in front of the shrine. Repairs were carried out from 2003 to 2010, mainly re-roofing and coloring, and the original coloring was restored. Designated as {{ill|lt=National Treasure|Q1139795|qid=Q1139795|1=National Treasure (Japan)}} in 2012<ref>{{PDFlink|[http://www.bunka.go.jp/ima/press_release/pdf/jubunkenzou_shittei_240521.pdf About the designation of national treasures and important cultural properties (buildings) (Agency for Cultural Affairs)]}}</ref>{{deadurl|date=July 2018}}<ref>Designation of National Treasures and Important Cultural Properties (Buildings) in 2012 ○From National Treasures (Buildings){{Cite book|Japanese book|title= White Paper on Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology Chapter 8 Part 2 / Trends and developments in education, science and technology policies - Realizing a nation built on culture and the arts |chapter=Designated on July 9, 2012 (1 item in total) Kangi-in Seitendo |url= https://www.mext.go.jp/b_menu/hakusho/html/hpab201301/1338525_019.pdf|format = PDF |page= 294|date= 2012-07-09|accessdate= 2018-07-10}}</ref><ref>Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology Notification No. 106 of July 9, 2012</ref><ref>{{Cite web|Japanese book|url= http://www.city.kumagaya.lg.jp/kanko/midokoro/menumasyoudenzan/kangiinkokuho.html |title=“Kangi-in Seitendo” designated as a national treasure|date= 2012-07-09|accessdate= 2012-07-12|publisher= Kumagaya City}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|Japanese book|others= Cultural Properties Protection Committee; Agency for Cultural Affairs Cultural Properties Protection Department |title = Newly designated cultural properties |work= Monthly cultural properties |publisher = Daiichi Hoki Publishing |number= 586|year = 2012|issn= 0016-5948 |ncid= AN00221652}}</ref>.

