=== Inarizushi in Menuma ===
[[File:Menuma Inari-zushi 01.JPG|thumb|200px|Menuma Inari-zushi]]
It has been eaten since the Edo period, mainly by visitors to our hospital. Kumagaya City Tourism Association official website|date=2014-10-02|access-date=2026-01-17|last=}}</ref>, {{ill|lt=Inari sushi|Q874397|qid=Q874397|1=Inarizushi}} is a specialty of the Menuma district (formerly {{ill|lt=Menuma Town|Q6817684|qid=Q6817684|1=Menuma, Saitama}}). It is characterized by its elongated shape, which is different from typical Inari sushi.Before the war, on the contrary, this elongated shape was common, but after the war, with the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=contract processing system|1=UNKNOWN}}, the standard was reduced to the size of nigiri sushi. However, in the Menuma area, efforts were made to maintain the elongated shape, resulting in the sushi being a specialty that continues to this day. To be a "jumbo"...Submit a report and keep the size Kumagaya's Jumbo Inari "100 Year Food" certification report meeting (Saitama Shimbun) - Yahoo! News]</ref>. In recent years, it has become popular as a specialty attached to the national treasure (main shrine).

{{ill|lt=Maki sushi|Q5739461|qid=Q5739461|1=Makizushi}} Three sushi restaurants specialize in this set (Sukeroku sushi), all of which originated as the teahouse in front of our hospital. <ref name=":0" /> operates within the hospital grounds and nearby areas. Similar products can also be found in surrounding areas, such as {{ill|lt=Roadside Station Menuma|Q11640968|qid=Q11640968|1=Roadside station Menuma}}'s ``{{ill|lt=Ginko Ogino|Q4794092|qid=Q4794092|lt=Gingin|1=Ogino Ginko}} Sushi.''On {{ill|lt=Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries|Q1376786|qid=Q1376786|1=Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries (Japan)}}'s "My Local Cuisine" website, it is introduced under the name "Shoten Sushi", which originates from our hospital.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Shoten Sushi Saitama Prefecture {{!}} Our local cuisine: Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries|url=https://www.maff.go.jp/j/keikaku/syokubunka/k_ryouri/search_menu/menu/33_29_saitama.html|website=www.maff.go.jp|access-date=2026-01-17}}</ref> etc.<ref group="Note">It is also sometimes written as "Shoten Sushi". It is presumed that all of these names are misspelled from the shop name  of the three shops mentioned above, which faces the temple grounds, and it seems inappropriate as a unified name for the three shops. </ref>In the past, there was no unified name, but as a tourist guide for Kumagaya City, it was advertised under the name ``''Menuma no Inarizushi''. Kumagaya City Tourism Association Official Homepage|date=2014-10-02|access-date=2026-01-17|last=}}</ref>, as this name has also been applied and registered in the 100-year food program mentioned below, in recent years, each of the 4 “Nagai Inari Sushi from the Menuma area” products, including Gingin Sushi, have been introduced.<ref name=":0" It is common to refer to the general term ``'''Menuma no Inarizushi''''.March 3, 2023, {{ill|lt=Agency for Cultural Affairs|Q1009519|qid=Q1009519|1=Agency for Cultural Affairs}} “{{ill|lt=100 Years of Food|Q134383966|qid=Q134383966|1=100 Years of Food}}” “100 Years of Traditional Food Category ～Local cuisine that has continued since the Edo period～” <ref>[https://foodculture2021.go.jp/wp/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/r4_100nenfood.pdf List of “100 Year Food” by prefecture (FY2020)]</ref>.

=== Transportation ===

=== car ===
*{{ill|lt=Gunma Prefectural Road/Saitama Prefectural Road No. 341 Ota Kumagaya Line|Q11609884|qid=Q11609884|lt=Prefectural Road No. 341 Ota Kumagaya Line|1=Gunma Prefectural Road and Saitama Prefectural Road Route 341}} Along. In addition, between the main hall and the main temple, {{ill|lt=Saitama Prefectural Road No. 59 Hanyu-Tsumuma Line|Q11427072|qid=Q11427072|lt=Prefectural Road No. 59 Hanyu-Tsumanum Line|1=Saitama Prefectural Road Route 59}} runs, and is called ``Enmusubi Dori'' (because this hospital is said to have {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=divine blessings|1=UNKNOWN}} for matchmaking).
*: There is a private parking lot nearby. However, the parking spaces facing the approach between the Ota Kumagaya Line and Kisomon Gate and the 6th parking lot behind the main shrine are not available for use by worshipers (please refer to the official website and local information).
*: Since the shrine is crowded during New Year's holidays and during spring and autumn festivals, Kumagaya City recommends parking at the Menuma Tourist Parking Lot in the south and taking a stroll around the shrine.

=== Bus ===
*{{ill|lt=Asahi Motors|Q11517584|qid=Q11517584|lt=Asahi Bus|1=Asahi Motor}}: {{ill|lt=Kumagaya Station|Q735594|qid=Q735594|1=Kumagaya Station}} North Exit Platform 6 - [KM61] {{ill|lt=Ota Station (Gunma Prefecture)|Q8081729|qid=Q8081729|lt=Ota Station|1=Ōta Station (Gunma)}} line/[KM63] Take the bus bound for {{ill|lt=Nishikoizumi Station|Q11627702|qid=Q11627702|1=Nishi-Koizumi Station}}/[KM64] Menuma Shotenmae<ref group="Notes">[KM65] Menuma, get off at the "Menuma Shimomachi" stop, and walk for 13 minutes. </ref>, get off at the "Menuma Shoten-mae" stop. *Late-night hours after 10pm depart from bus stop 3 (destinations above + [KM62] bound for Nishiyajima are also available)
*{{ill|lt=Yu-Yu Bus (Kumagaya City)|Q11280693|qid=Q11280693|lt=Yu-Yu Bus|1=Kumagaya City Community Bus}} (Community Bus): Glider (Kumagaya Station South Exit - Menuma Administrative Center), Glider Wagon (Menuma Circulation), get off at the "Menuma Shoten-mae" stop or the "Shotenzan Minami Entrance" stop.

